## 4. Descriptor Gap Analysis

Applying the Descriptor Gap Principle to identify everything required. Any gap found below represents a module that must exist.

### 4.1 Language Processing Pipeline Gaps

Starting from source text (P) to running program (S), every transformation step is a Descriptor that constrains the substrate:

| Gap / Descriptor | Module Required | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| What are the valid tokens? | `etpl_tokens` | Defines all token types |
| How do we extract tokens from source? | `etpl_lexer` | Character-level scanner |
| How do we parse token sequences? | `etpl_parser` | Grammar rules |
| What are the AST node shapes? | `etpl_ast` | Node type definitions |
| How do we resolve names/types? | `etpl_semantic` | Scope, type checking |
| How do we execute the AST directly? | `etpl_interpreter` | Tree-walking eval |
| How do we emit .etb bytecode? | `etpl_compiler` | AST → bytecode |
| What is the .etb format? | `etpl_binary_format` | Header + sections + opcodes |
| How do we execute .etb? | `etpl_vm` | Bytecode executor |
| How do we generate native machine code? | `etpl_codegen` | .etb → native binary |
| What math operations exist? | `etpl_math` | Pure ET math, no imports |
| What types does the language have? | `etpl_types` | Type system |
| What is in the standard library? | `etpl_stdlib` | Built-in operations |
| How does the module system work? | `etpl_module` | @ETPL:import |
| How do we provide an interactive shell? | `etpl_repl` | REPL |
| How do we provide a CLI? | `etpl_cli` | Command dispatch |
| How do we translate other languages? | `etpl_translator` | Python/C→ETPL |
| How do we bootstrap without imports? | `etpl_bootstrap` | The zero-import entry point |

