## 6. The .etb Binary Format Specification

The Exception Theory Binary (.etb) is the native compiled format of ETPL. It requires no external toolchain to execute — only the ETPL VM. It also serves as the intermediate representation for native code generation.

**ET Derivation of the Format:**
- P_etb = the raw byte stream (substrate, featureless without the format descriptors)
- D_etb = the section table, magic bytes, opcode set (the constraints that impose meaning)
- T_etb = the VM executor (the traverser that navigates the binary)

### 6.1 File Magic and Header

```
Offset  Size  Field          Value / Description
------  ----  -----          -------------------
0x00    4     magic          0x45 0x54 0x42 0x31  ("ETB1") — Exception Theory Binary v1
0x04    2     version_major  uint16_le — format major version
0x06    2     version_minor  uint16_le — format minor version
0x08    4     flags          uint32_le — bit flags (see below)
0x0C    4     section_count  uint32_le — number of sections
0x10    4     entry_point    uint32_le — offset of entry in .code section
0x14    4     manifold_sym   uint32_le — must be 12 (MANIFOLD_SYMMETRY)
0x18    8     source_hash    uint64_le — hash of source .pdt file
0x20    4     checksum       uint32_le — header checksum (XOR of all prior fields)
0x24    ...   section_table  array of SectionEntry (12 bytes each, max 12 sections)
```

**Header Flags (bit field):**
```
Bit 0: SELF_CONTAINED — no external module references
Bit 1: DEBUG_INFO     — debug symbols included
Bit 2: OPTIMISED      — compiler optimisation applied
Bit 3: NATIVE_ONLY    — bytecode stripped, only native stub present
Bit 4: ET_VERIFIED    — all ET invariants verified at compile time
Bits 5-31: reserved = 0
```

**Section Entry (12 bytes):**
```
Offset  Size  Field
------  ----  -----
0x00    4     section_type   (see section types below)
0x04    4     offset         uint32_le — byte offset from start of file
0x08    4     length         uint32_le — byte length of section
```

**Section Types:**
```
0x01  CODE     — ET bytecode instructions
0x02  DATA     — constants, string table, numeric literals
0x03  SYMBOLS  — function/variable name table
0x04  IMPORTS  — module dependency list
0x05  EXPORTS  — public symbol table
0x06  TYPES    — type descriptor table
0x07  LATTICE  — embedded ET lattice data (constants, ratios)
0x08  DEBUG    — source map, line numbers
0x09  NATIVE   — platform-native machine code (optional)
0x0A  META     — ET metadata (module name, author, version)
0x0B  RELOC    — relocation table (for native section)
0x0C  MANIFEST — module manifest (@ETPL: directives)
```

**Maximum 12 sections: derived from MANIFOLD_SYMMETRY = 12.**

### 6.2 The ET Bytecode Instruction Set

All opcodes are derived from the PDT structure. Opcodes are 1 byte; operands follow as needed (variable width, ET-encoded).

**Format:** `[opcode: 1 byte] [arg_count: 1 byte] [args: variable]`

#### Category P — Point / Substrate Operations (substrate manipulation)
```
0x10  P_LOAD_CONST     1 arg: index into DATA section
0x11  P_LOAD_LOCAL     1 arg: local slot index
0x12  P_STORE_LOCAL    1 arg: local slot index
0x13  P_LOAD_GLOBAL    1 arg: symbol index
0x14  P_STORE_GLOBAL   1 arg: symbol index
0x15  P_ALLOC          1 arg: size in ET-units
0x16  P_FREE           0 args
0x17  P_COPY           0 args
0x18  P_SWAP           0 args
0x19  P_DUP            0 args
0x1A  P_POP            0 args
0x1B  P_LOAD_ATTR      1 arg: attribute name index
0x1C  P_STORE_ATTR     1 arg: attribute name index
0x1D  P_BUILD_LIST     1 arg: element count
0x1E  P_BUILD_MANIFOLD 1 arg: field count
0x1F  P_INDEX          0 args — stack: [P, D] → P[D]
```

#### Category D — Descriptor / Constraint Operations (apply constraints)
```
0x20  D_BIND           0 args — binds top two stack items (P ∘ D)
0x21  D_ADD            0 args — arithmetic descriptor addition
0x22  D_SUB            0 args
0x23  D_MUL            0 args
0x24  D_DIV            0 args
0x25  D_MOD            0 args
0x26  D_POW            0 args
0x27  D_NEG            0 args
0x28  D_ABS            0 args
0x29  D_EQ             0 args
0x2A  D_NEQ            0 args
0x2B  D_LT             0 args
0x2C  D_LE             0 args
0x2D  D_GT             0 args
0x2E  D_GE             0 args
0x2F  D_AND            0 args — logical
0x30  D_OR             0 args
0x31  D_NOT            0 args
0x32  D_XOR            0 args
0x33  D_BAND           0 args — bitwise
0x34  D_BOR            0 args
0x35  D_BNOT           0 args
0x36  D_BXOR           0 args
0x37  D_LSHIFT         0 args
0x38  D_RSHIFT         0 args
0x39  D_COMPOSE        0 args — ∘ operator (P∘D, D∘T, P∘D∘T)
0x3A  D_COERCE         1 arg: target type index
0x3B  D_VALIDATE       1 arg: constraint index (type-check)
0x3C  D_RANGE          0 args — descriptor range [lo, hi]
0x3D  D_LATTICE_STEP   1 arg: step_count (semitone lattice traversal)
0x3E  D_KOIDE          0 args — apply Koide ratio (2/3) as descriptor weight
0x3F  D_BASE_VAR       0 args — apply BASE_VARIANCE (1/12) as descriptor
```

#### Category T — Traverser / Agency Operations (navigate and act)
```
0x40  T_CALL           2 args: arg_count, symbol_index
0x41  T_CALL_DYNAMIC   1 arg: arg_count (callee on stack)
0x42  T_RETURN         0 args
0x43  T_RETURN_VAL     0 args
0x44  T_JUMP           1 arg: relative offset (int16)
0x45  T_JUMP_IF        1 arg: relative offset (int16)
0x46  T_JUMP_UNLESS    1 arg: relative offset (int16)
0x47  T_TRAVERSE       0 args — iterate: advance traverser over collection
0x48  T_TRAVERSE_DONE  1 arg: jump offset if exhausted
0x49  T_RECURSE        1 arg: depth limit (MANIFOLD_SYMMETRY² = 144 max)
0x4A  T_SPAWN          1 arg: function index (concurrent traverser)
0x4B  T_YIELD          0 args
0x4C  T_AWAIT          0 args
0x4D  T_BIND_T         0 args — bind Traverser to (P∘D) on stack
0x4E  T_UNBIND_T       0 args
0x4F  T_EXCEPTION_PUSH 1 arg: handler offset
0x50  T_EXCEPTION_POP  0 args
0x51  T_RAISE          0 args
0x52  T_GROUND         0 args — force Exception state (grounding)
0x53  T_INCOHERENCE    0 args — signal Incoherence (forbidden config)
0x54  T_MEDIATE        0 args — explicit Mediation step
0x55  T_SHIMMER        0 args — T navigates indeterminately (non-deterministic choice)
0x56  T_SUBSTANTIATE   0 args — commit a (P∘D) config with T → Exception
0x57  T_LOAD_SELF      0 args — meta-traversal: load own traversal state
```

#### Category S — Something / System Operations (output and meta)
```
0x60  S_PRINT          1 arg: format_index
0x61  S_INPUT          0 args
0x62  S_OPEN_FILE      1 arg: mode
0x63  S_CLOSE_FILE     0 args
0x64  S_READ_FILE      0 args
0x65  S_WRITE_FILE     0 args
0x66  S_SYSCALL        1 arg: syscall_number (direct bare-metal)
0x67  S_HALT           1 arg: exit_code
0x68  S_MODULE_LOAD    1 arg: module_index
0x69  S_MODULE_CALL    2 args: module_index, symbol_index
0x6A  S_ET_VERIFY      0 args — verify ET invariants (assert PDT completeness)
0x6B  S_DEBUG_TRACE    0 args — emit debug trace if debug flag set
0x6C  S_MEM_READ       0 args — bare-metal memory read
0x6D  S_MEM_WRITE      0 args — bare-metal memory write
```

**Total: 94 opcodes across 4 categories (P, D, T, S = E, I, M, S)**

The four opcode categories directly mirror the EIM=PDT identity:
- P opcodes → Exception operations (grounding/substrate)
- D opcodes → Incoherence boundary operations (constraints/filtering)
- T opcodes → Mediation operations (agency/traversal)
- S opcodes → Something operations (output/existence)

### 6.3 Native Code Generation Targets

The .etb format supports optional native machine code in the NATIVE section. The code generator in `etpl_codegen` can target:

| Target | ABI | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| x86-64 Linux | System V AMD64 | Primary POSIX target |
| x86-64 Windows | Microsoft x64 | PE32+ format |
| x86-64 Bare Metal | Custom ET ABI | No OS; direct hardware |
| ARM64 Linux | AARCH64 ELF | Raspberry Pi, Apple M-series |
| ARM64 Bare Metal | Custom ET ABI | Embedded systems |
| RISC-V 64 | ELF | Open-source processors |

The ET ABI (bare metal) uses:
- ET calling convention: P-registers (substrate) = rdi/rsi/rdx/rcx/r8/r9; D-registers (descriptor arguments) = r10/r11; T-register (traverser/return) = rax
- Stack grows downward; frame aligned to 12 bytes (MANIFOLD_SYMMETRY)
- Syscalls via direct int 0x80 (Linux) or BIOS (bare metal)

