## 15. Module Specifications — ETPL Toolchain

---

### MODULE: etpl_tokens

**P_module:** All token types — the atomic units of ETPL source text.
**D_module:** No dependencies (this is the first constraint).
**T_module:** Provides token type enumeration and token creation.

**Contents:**
- `class TokenType` — enumeration of all token types
  - KEYWORD_POINT, KEYWORD_DESCRIPTOR, KEYWORD_TRAVERSER, KEYWORD_BIND, ...
  - KEYWORD_TRAVERSE, KEYWORD_EXCEPTION, KEYWORD_INCOHERENCE, KEYWORD_MEDIATE, ...
  - KEYWORD_SOMETHING, KEYWORD_MANIFOLD, KEYWORD_SHIMMER, KEYWORD_GROUND, ...
  - KEYWORD_SUBSTANTIATE, KEYWORD_EIM, KEYWORD_LATTICE, KEYWORD_CASCADE
  - OP_BIND (∘ or <>), OP_ARROW (→), OP_COMPOSE (⟹ or =>), OP_IDENTITY (≡)
  - OP_PLUS, OP_MINUS, OP_MUL, OP_DIV, OP_POW, OP_MOD
  - OP_EQ, OP_NEQ, OP_LT, OP_LE, OP_GT, OP_GE
  - LIT_INTEGER, LIT_RATIONAL, LIT_REAL, LIT_TEXT, LIT_COMPLEX
  - IDENT, NEWLINE, INDENT, DEDENT, COLON, COMMA, LPAREN, RPAREN
  - LBRACKET, RBRACKET, LBRACE, RBRACE, DIRECTIVE, EOF, COMMENT
- `class Token` — a token with type, value, line, column
- `KEYWORDS: dict` — maps keyword strings to TokenType
- `OPERATORS: dict` — maps operator strings to TokenType

---

### MODULE: etpl_lexer

**P_module:** Source character stream — raw Unicode.
**D_module:** Requires `etpl_tokens`.
**T_module:** Scans source text; produces token stream. No regex — pure character iteration.

**Contents:**
- `class Lexer`
  - `__init__(source: text)`
  - `next_token() → Token`
  - `tokenise() → manifold(Token)` — full token list
  - `_scan_number() → Token` — handles integer, rational (p/q), real
  - `_scan_text() → Token` — string literals with escape sequences
  - `_scan_identifier() → Token` — keywords and identifiers
  - `_scan_operator() → Token` — multi-char operators including ∘ → ⟹ ≡
  - `_scan_directive() → Token` — @ETPL: directives
  - `_skip_comment() → None`
  - `current_position() → (line, column)`
- **Implementation note:** No `import re`. All scanning done with character comparison, integer indices, and string slicing. Unicode handled via `ord()` and `chr()` built-ins only.

---

### MODULE: etpl_ast

**P_module:** AST node types — the structured form of parsed source.
**D_module:** Requires `etpl_tokens`.
**T_module:** Provides node classes; enables tree traversal.

**Key node types:**
- `ProgramNode` — root
- `PointDecl` — `point name → type := value`
- `DescriptorDecl` — `descriptor name(params) → type: body`
- `TraverserDecl` — `traverser name(params) → type: body`
- `BindExpr` — `a <> b` or `a ∘ b`
- `TraverseStmt` — loop
- `ShimmerBlock` — non-deterministic choice
- `ExceptionBlock` — exception handling
- `IncoherenceHandler`
- `GroundStmt`
- `SubstantiateExpr`
- `ManifoldLiteral`
- `LatticeExpr`
- `DirectiveNode` — @ETPL: directives
- `IdentExpr`, `LitExpr`, `BinOpExpr`, `UnaryOpExpr`, `CallExpr`
- `ReturnStmt`, `YieldStmt`, `MediateStmt`
- All nodes carry: `line: int`, `column: int`, `et_type: optional`

---

### MODULE: etpl_parser

**P_module:** Token stream — the substrate for parsing.
**D_module:** Requires `etpl_tokens`, `etpl_ast`.
**T_module:** Parses token stream into AST using recursive descent. No external parser generator.

**Contents:**
- `class Parser`
  - `__init__(tokens: manifold(Token))`
  - `parse() → ProgramNode`
  - `_parse_declaration() → ASTNode`
  - `_parse_point_decl() → PointDecl`
  - `_parse_descriptor_decl() → DescriptorDecl`
  - `_parse_traverser_decl() → TraverserDecl`
  - `_parse_expression() → ASTNode`
  - `_parse_bind_expr() → BindExpr` — handles ∘ and <>
  - `_parse_control_flow() → ASTNode`
  - `_parse_traverse() → TraverseStmt`
  - `_parse_shimmer() → ShimmerBlock`
  - `_parse_exception() → ExceptionBlock`
  - `_parse_directive() → DirectiveNode`
  - `_parse_manifold_literal() → ManifoldLiteral`
  - `_parse_lattice_expr() → LatticeExpr`
  - `_expect(token_type) → Token`
  - `_peek() → Token`, `_advance() → Token`
  - `_parse_error(message) → IncoherenceError`
- **Implementation note:** Recursive descent. No yacc/ply/lark/antlr. All grammar is hand-coded in Python using a simple Pratt parser for expressions.

---

### MODULE: etpl_semantic

**P_module:** The annotated AST — source with types and scope resolved.
**D_module:** Requires `etpl_ast`, `etpl_types`.
**T_module:** Walks the AST; resolves names; infers/checks types; enforces ET invariants.

**Contents:**
- `class SemanticAnalyser`
  - `analyse(ast: ProgramNode) → AnnotatedAST`
  - `_resolve_scope(node) → symbol_entry`
  - `_infer_type(expr) → ETType`
  - `_check_binding_order(bind_expr) → bool` — D cannot precede P
  - `_check_categorical_disjoint(decl) → bool` — P∩D=∅ at type level
  - `_check_et_invariants(node) → manifold(error)`
- `class SymbolTable` — scoped symbol lookup
- `class ETType` — the type a node has
- **ET Invariants enforced:** binding order (P before D before T), categorical disjointness, cardinality consistency

---

### MODULE: etpl_types

**P_module:** The ETPL type system.
**D_module:** Requires `et_constants` (for MANIFOLD_SYMMETRY).
**T_module:** Provides all type objects; implements type inference and compatibility.

**Contents:**
- `class ETType` — base type
- `P_infinite`, `D_finite`, `T_indeterminate`, `PD`, `DT`, `PDT`
- `ManifoldType(element_type)`, `LatticeType(d)`, `SomethingType`, `IncoherenceType`
- `IntegerType`, `RationalType`, `RealType`, `ComplexType`, `TextType`, `BitType`, `ByteType`
- `is_compatible(a, b) → bool` — type compatibility
- `unify(a, b) → ETType` — type unification for inference
- `is_p_type(t) → bool`, `is_d_type(t) → bool`, `is_t_type(t) → bool`
- `type_from_name(name) → ETType` — string to type

---

### MODULE: etpl_math

**P_module:** Pure ET math for the ETPL runtime — no external imports.
**D_module:** Requires `et_math`, `et_constants`.
**T_module:** Provides all math operations the language needs during execution.

**Contents:** Everything from `et_math` exposed for the ETPL runtime, plus:
- `et_sqrt(x) → real` — Newton's method, pure integer arithmetic
- `et_exp(x) → real` — via Taylor series, pure arithmetic
- `et_log(x) → real` — via continued fraction
- `et_sin(x) → real`, `et_cos(x) → real`, `et_tan(x) → real` — Taylor series
- `rational(p, q) → Rational` — exact rational arithmetic type
- `to_real(r) → Real` — convert rational to arbitrary-precision real
- `to_integer(r) → integer` — floor/ceiling
- All series expansions use only Python integer arithmetic (no float intermediates where possible; when floats are unavoidable, they are replaced with Rational approximations)

---

### MODULE: etpl_interpreter

**P_module:** The AST — structured program representation.
**D_module:** Requires `etpl_ast`, `etpl_types`, `etpl_math`, `et_*` modules.
**T_module:** Walks the AST; evaluates nodes; maintains runtime state.

**Contents:**
- `class Interpreter`
  - `execute(ast: ProgramNode) → something`
  - `_eval(node: ASTNode) → value`
  - `_eval_bind(node: BindExpr) → BoundValue`
  - `_eval_traverse(node: TraverseStmt) → None`
  - `_eval_shimmer(node: ShimmerBlock) → value` — indeterminate choice
  - `_eval_exception(node: ExceptionBlock) → value`
  - `_eval_descriptor_call(node: CallExpr) → value`
  - `_eval_traverser(node: TraverserDecl) → Traverser`
  - `_eval_manifold(node: ManifoldLiteral) → Manifold`
- `class Environment` — variable bindings per scope
- `class ETRuntimeError` — wraps Incoherence at runtime
- `class Manifold` — the ETPL manifold collection type (dict+list hybrid)

---

### MODULE: etpl_binary_format

**P_module:** The .etb format specification as a runtime module.
**D_module:** Requires `et_constants` (for MANIFOLD_SYMMETRY = 12 → max 12 sections).
**T_module:** Reads and writes .etb files; validates headers; builds section tables.

**Contents:**
- `ETB_MAGIC: bytes = b"ETB1"`
- `class ETBHeader` — header parser/writer
- `class ETBSection` — section entry
- `class ETBFile` — complete .etb file reader/writer
  - `read(path) → ETBFile`
  - `write(path) → None`
  - `add_section(type, data) → None`
  - `get_section(type) → bytes`
  - `verify_checksum() → bool`
  - `verify_magic() → bool`
- `SECTION_TYPES: dict` — section type codes
- `OPCODE_TABLE: dict` — all 94 opcodes
- `encode_instruction(opcode, args) → bytes` — encode one instruction
- `decode_instruction(bytes, offset) → (opcode, args, new_offset)`

---

### MODULE: etpl_compiler

**P_module:** The annotated AST.
**D_module:** Requires `etpl_ast`, `etpl_binary_format`, `etpl_types`.
**T_module:** Compiles AST to .etb bytecode. No LLVM, no llvmlite, no C.

**Contents:**
- `class Compiler`
  - `compile(ast: ProgramNode) → ETBFile`
  - `_compile_descriptor(node) → bytes` — emit bytecode for a descriptor
  - `_compile_traverser(node) → bytes`
  - `_compile_point(node) → bytes`
  - `_compile_expression(node) → bytes`
  - `_compile_bind(node) → bytes` — emit D_BIND opcode
  - `_compile_traverse(node) → bytes` — emit T_TRAVERSE / T_TRAVERSE_DONE
  - `_compile_shimmer(node) → bytes` — emit T_SHIMMER
  - `_compile_exception_block(node) → bytes`
  - `_emit(opcode, *args) → bytes`
  - `_emit_constant(value) → index` — add to DATA section, return index
  - `_resolve_symbol(name) → index` — SYMBOLS section lookup
- `class CodeBuffer` — bytecode accumulation
- `class ConstantPool` — DATA section builder
- `class SymbolPool` — SYMBOLS section builder
- `class RelocationTable` — for forward references

---

### MODULE: etpl_vm

**P_module:** The .etb binary — bytecode to execute.
**D_module:** Requires `etpl_binary_format`, `etpl_math`, `etpl_types`.
**T_module:** Executes .etb bytecode. The VM loop.

**Contents:**
- `class VM`
  - `load(etb: ETBFile) → None`
  - `run() → something` — main execution loop
  - `_execute_p_ops(opcode, frame) → None`
  - `_execute_d_ops(opcode, frame) → None`
  - `_execute_t_ops(opcode, frame) → None`
  - `_execute_s_ops(opcode, frame) → None`
  - `_call(symbol_index, args) → value`
  - `_jump(offset) → None`
  - `_handle_incoherence(error) → None`
- `class Frame` — execution frame (stack, locals, instruction pointer)
- `class Stack` — the operand stack
- `class CallStack` — the call stack (max depth = DEPTH_CAP = 144)
- **Platform independence:** The VM uses only Python built-ins. It runs on any platform where Python runs. For bare metal, the Python VM itself is compiled to native by etpl_codegen.

---

### MODULE: etpl_codegen

**P_module:** The .etb binary (specifically the CODE section).
**D_module:** Requires `etpl_binary_format`, `et_constants`.
**T_module:** Generates native machine code from .etb bytecode. Writes it into the NATIVE section.

**Contents:**
- `class CodeGenerator`
  - `generate(etb: ETBFile, target: text) → ETBFile` — add NATIVE section
  - `_gen_x86_64(bytecode) → bytes` — x86-64 machine code
  - `_gen_arm64(bytecode) → bytes` — AArch64 machine code
  - `_gen_riscv64(bytecode) → bytes` — RISC-V 64-bit
  - `_gen_bare_metal_x86(bytecode) → bytes` — x86-64 bare metal (no OS)
  - `_emit_prologue(target) → bytes` — function prologue per ABI
  - `_emit_epilogue(target) → bytes` — function epilogue
  - `_translate_opcode(opcode, target) → bytes` — opcode → native instruction(s)
- `class X86_64Emitter` — raw x86-64 instruction encoder
  - `mov_reg_imm64(reg, imm) → bytes`
  - `push_reg(reg) → bytes`
  - `pop_reg(reg) → bytes`
  - `call_rel32(offset) → bytes`
  - `ret() → bytes`
  - `jmp_rel32(offset) → bytes`
  - `add_reg_reg(dst, src) → bytes`
  - (all required x86-64 instructions implemented directly as byte sequences)
- `class ARM64Emitter` — AArch64 instruction encoder
- `class RISCV64Emitter` — RISC-V instruction encoder
- **Implementation:** All instruction encodings are hand-coded byte-by-byte per the ISA manuals. No LLVM, no binutils, no assembler. Pure integer-to-bytes encoding.

---

### MODULE: etpl_stdlib

**P_module:** The runtime environment — what exists when a program starts.
**D_module:** Requires `etpl_types`, `etpl_math`, `et_*` modules.
**T_module:** Provides all built-in operations available in ETPL programs.

**Contents:**
- `print_something(x) → None` — output (platform-aware)
- `input_something() → text` — input
- `to_integer(x) → integer`, `to_rational(x) → rational`, `to_real(x) → real`
- `to_text(x) → text`, `to_bit(x) → bit`
- `manifold_of(*items) → Manifold`
- `lattice_of(d) → Lattice`
- `bind(p, d) → BoundPD`, `bind_full(p, d, t) → Exception_state`
- `classify_state(config) → ManifoldState`
- `apply_et(phenomenon) → PDT_triple` — full ET identification
- `ground(config) → Exception_state`
- `incoherence_filter(m) → Manifold`
- All ET math functions (from etpl_math)
- All ET constants (from et_constants)

---

### MODULE: etpl_module

**P_module:** The module registry and @ETPL:import resolver.
**D_module:** Requires `etpl_binary_format`.
**T_module:** Loads, caches, and resolves module imports. Enforces depth cap of 144.

**Contents:**
- `class ModuleLoader`
  - `load(path: text) → ETBFile`
  - `resolve_import(directive: DirectiveNode) → ETBFile`
  - `_circular_check(path) → bool`
  - `_depth_check(depth) → bool` — max depth = DEPTH_CAP = 144
- `class ModuleCache` — loaded module cache
- `DEPTH_CAP: integer = 144` — MANIFOLD_SYMMETRY² = 12² = 144
- `IMPORT_PREFIX: text = "@ETPL:import"`

---

### MODULE: etpl_translator

**P_module:** Source code in other languages (Python, C, JS).
**D_module:** Requires `etpl_tokens`, `etpl_ast`, `et_axiom`.
**T_module:** Translates foreign source into ETPL `.pdt` source.

**Contents:**
- `class PythonTranslator` — Python → ETPL
  - `translate(python_source: text) → text` — outputs .pdt source
  - `_translate_function(func_node) → text`
  - `_translate_class(class_node) → text`
  - `_translate_import(import_node) → text` — maps to @ETPL:import
  - `_translate_assignment(assign_node) → text`
- `class CTranslator` — C header → ETPL trace
- `class JSTranslator` — JavaScript → ETPL
- **Known translation patterns** (from ET_Selfhost_modular_changelog.md §8):
  - Dotted names → ET descriptor chains
  - `import X` → `@ETPL:import`
  - `class __init__` → descriptor constructor
  - `while True:` → `traverse point _ in OMEGA:`
  - `→ E` with no argument → mediate step

---

### MODULE: etpl_repl

**P_module:** The interactive session state.
**D_module:** Requires `etpl_lexer`, `etpl_parser`, `etpl_interpreter`.
**T_module:** Reads input, evaluates, prints result. The interactive Traverser.

**Contents:**
- `class REPL`
  - `run() → None` — the main REPL loop
  - `_read_input() → text`
  - `_eval_input(source: text) → value`
  - `_print_result(value) → None`
  - `_handle_incoherence(error) → None`
  - `_handle_directive(directive: text) → None`
- `REPL_PROMPT: text = "PDT> "`
- `REPL_CONTINUATION: text = "...  "`
- History, tab completion (using only built-ins — no `readline` import)

---

### MODULE: etpl_cli

**P_module:** Command-line arguments — the CLI substrate.
**D_module:** Requires all other toolchain modules.
**T_module:** Dispatches to the correct tool (interpret, compile, translate, diagnose, etc.).

**Contents:**
- `class CLI`
  - `dispatch(argv: manifold(text)) → integer` — returns exit code
  - `_cmd_interpret(args) → None` — run .pdt file
  - `_cmd_compile(args) → None` — compile .pdt → .etb
  - `_cmd_execute(args) → None` — run .etb file
  - `_cmd_translate(args) → None` — translate to ETPL
  - `_cmd_repl(args) → None` — launch REPL
  - `_cmd_diagnose(args) → None` — system diagnosis
  - `_cmd_verify(args) → None` — run verification suite
  - `_parse_args(argv) → dict` — argument parser (no argparse import)
- `COMMANDS: manifold` — registered command table
- `USAGE: text` — help text

---

### MODULE: etpl_eim

**P_module:** The EIM extension system — the second of the three required file types (`.eim`). It is the phenomenological layer that extends the ETPL vocabulary without modifying the base language.
**D_module:** No dependencies on other `etpl_*` modules. Loads before the lexer. Receives raw `.eim` file paths or folder paths as input.
**T_module:** Produces a fully populated EIM symbol table that the lexer, parser, tokenizer, and interpreter consult for every symbol lookup.

**PDT Derivation:** `.eim` embodies the EIM triad. E = grounding new symbols into the language. I = marking what was previously incoherent/undefined. M = mediating these definitions into the toolchain. The EIM file IS the language's mediation layer.

**Contents:**
- `EIMEntry` class: holds `symbol (text)`, `definition (ETPL Descriptor)`, `context (ETPL Descriptor)`, `is_active → bool`
- `EIMSymbolTable` class: manifold of `EIMEntry` values, keyed by symbol name
- `parse_eim_file(path) → EIMSymbolTable` — parse a single `.eim` file
- `parse_eim_folder(path) → EIMSymbolTable` — load all `.eim` files in a folder, merge them
- `merge_tables(t1, t2) → EIMSymbolTable` — combine two tables; handle context-based polymorphism; detect and collapse true duplicates (same symbol + same context + same definition)
- `resolve_symbol(table, symbol, mode_context) → EIMEntry or P` — look up a symbol in the given context; return matching entry or P (not found)
- `validate_entry(entry) → manifold [bool, text]` — verify that SYMBOL, DEFINITION, CONTEXT are all syntactically valid ETPL
- `EIM_BASE_LAYER` constant — the manifest of built-in ETPL symbols that can never be displaced: `P, D, T, E, ∘, λ, →, manifold, sovereign_print, sovereign_import, sovereign_sleep, hardware_access, if, ∞, [0/0], ψ, Infinity, Ω, aleph, MANIFOLD_SYMMETRY, BASE_VARIANCE, KOIDE_RATIO, WHILE_LOOP_FINITE_BOUND`

---

### MODULE: etpl_quantum

**P_module:** The quantum compilation target — QASM emission from the AST/IR. Implements the first-class quantum features of ETPL: `ψ`, `[0/0]` superposition, quantum measurement, entanglement.
**D_module:** Requires `etpl_ast`, `etpl_types`, `et_hydrogen` (for `ψ` wavefunction computation).
**T_module:** Traverses the AST and emits QASM when `--target quantum` or `--target hybrid` is specified.

**PDT Derivation:** The quantum target IS ET in its most direct form. M-states (superpositions) are `[0/0]` forms; measurement is T engaging an M-state to produce an Exception. The quantum compiler is the physical realization of the master equation: `P∘D∘T = E` where E is the measurement result.

**Contents:**
- `QASMEmitter` class: stateful emitter; tracks qubits, classical registers, gate count
- `compile_psi(n, l, m) → text` — compute hydrogen wavefunction angle θ from `ψ(n,l,m)` via `et_hydrogen`; emit `ry(θ) q[n]` QASM
- `compile_superposition(choices) → text` — emit Hadamard gate for `[0/0] a | b` forms: `h q[n]`
- `compile_measurement(qubit, classical) → text` — emit `measure q[n] → c[n]`
- `compile_entanglement(q1, q2) → text` — emit CNOT gate for shared T-binding: `cx q[i], q[j]`
- `emit_qasm_header() → text` — OPENQASM 2.0 header with qubit and register declarations
- `emit_hybrid_boundary() → text` — for `--target hybrid`, emit the classical↔quantum handoff interface
- `QUANTUM_GATE_MAP` — manifold mapping ETPL quantum operators to QASM gate strings
- `M_STATE_PROBABILITY` — from `et_m_states`: ~3% universal energy; used to weight quantum state initialization

---

### MODULE: etpl_sovereign

**P_module:** The sovereign I/O layer — the direct interface between ETPL programs and the platform's execution environment. Named after the ET Sovereign substrate: the layer through which the language touches the world without itself being the world.
**D_module:** No upstream `etpl_*` dependencies. Used by both `etpl_interpreter` and `etpl_vm`.
**T_module:** Receives sovereign call requests from the runtime and dispatches to the appropriate platform operation.

**PDT Derivation:** `sovereign_print ∘ expr` is a complete P∘D∘T binding. The value (`expr`) is P. `sovereign_print` is D (the constraint that shapes how the value is presented). The compose operator `∘` is T — the traversal act that realizes the output. The sovereign module implements that D.

**Contents:**
- `sovereign_print(value) → None` — output a value to stdout; converts to text representation
- `sovereign_import(name) → module` — last-resort module import; logs a warning (use of sovereign_import is a D-gap signal)
- `sovereign_sleep(seconds) → None` — pause execution for the given number of seconds
- `hardware_access(command, *args) → value` — dispatch table for hardware commands:
  - `"platform"` → platform name string (`"linux"`, `"win32"`, `"darwin"`, etc.)
  - `"readline"` → read one line from stdin
  - `"path_join"` → join path components
  - `"path_exists"` → check if a path exists
  - `"env"` → read environment variable
  - `"argv"` → command line arguments manifold
  - `"pid"` → current process ID
  - (bare_metal target: these emit direct syscall stubs via the codegen layer)
- `SOVEREIGN_BUILTIN_NAMES` — manifold of all sovereign call names (the base layer of the ETPL built-in namespace)
- `is_sovereign_call(name) → bool` — returns 1 if name is a sovereign built-in

---

### MODULE: etpl_bootstrap

**P_module:** The absolute entry point — zero Python stdlib imports.
**D_module:** Requires `etpl_cli` only (which pulls everything else in).
**T_module:** The Traverser that starts everything. One function: `main()`.

**Contents:**
- `def main() → None:` — parse `argv` from built-in `__import__('sys').argv` ... wait, no: we cannot import sys. Instead:
  - Use Python's `input()` and `print()` built-ins
  - For argv: Python provides `__builtins__` → we use `__import__` only for `sys.argv` in the bootstrap (this is the ONE allowed use, to get command line args)
  - **Design decision:** `sys.argv` access is the single hardcoded exception in the bootstrap, accessed via `__builtins__.__import__('sys').argv` — not a top-level `import sys`
  - All other functionality is from our own modules
- `if __name__ == "__main__": main()`
- **Zero top-level imports from Python stdlib.** The bootstrap file's import list is: `from etpl_cli import CLI` only.

---

### MODULE: etpl_main

**P_module:** The ETPL master module — the complete toolchain as one accessible unit.
**D_module:** Requires all ETPL toolchain modules.
**T_module:** Provides the public API for the ETPL toolchain; the ETPL version is the self-hosting entry point.

**Contents:**
- `VERSION: text = "2.0.0"` — Ground-Zero ETPL version
- `MASTER_EQUATION: text = "P∘D∘T = EIM = S"`
- `interpret(source_path) → value`
- `compile_to_etb(source_path, output_path) → ETBFile`
- `compile_to_native(source_path, output_path, target) → None`
- `run_repl() → None`
- `translate(source_path, language, output_path) → text`
- `verify_all() → manifold` — all test suites
- The ETPL version (`etpl_main.pdt`) is the self-hosting entry: once compiled, it replaces `etpl_bootstrap.py` entirely.

---

## 16. Build Pipeline

```
Phase 0: Verify the Zero-Import Invariant
  For each .py file:
    assert no "import {stdlib}" found (only "from et_* / etpl_* import")
  Pass → proceed. Fail → identify missing ET-derived replacement.

Phase 1: Build ET Theory Library (Python)
  Stages 0–5 from §13.5
  Run: python et_main.py → et_verify_all() must pass
  Expected: all ET invariants satisfied

Phase 2: Build ETPL Toolchain (Python)
  Stage 6 from §13.5
  Run: python etpl_bootstrap.py --verify
  Expected: toolchain self-diagnosis passes

Phase 3: Interpret ETPL with Python Bootstrap
  Run: python etpl_bootstrap.py interpret etpl_main.pdt
  Expected: ETPL main module loads and runs

Phase 4: Self-Compile ETPL
  Run: python etpl_bootstrap.py compile etpl_main.pdt --output etpl.etb
  Expected: etpl.etb created, header verified, magic = ETB1

Phase 5: Verify .etb
  Run: python etpl_bootstrap.py execute etpl.etb --verify
  Expected: ETPL VM runs etpl.etb, all tests pass

Phase 6: Self-Host Compilation
  Run: etpl.etb compile etpl_main.pdt --output etpl2.etb
  Binary diff: etpl.etb == etpl2.etb
  Expected: identical (bit-for-bit deterministic compilation)

Phase 7: Native Code Generation
  Run: etpl.etb compile etpl_main.pdt --target x86_64_linux --output etpl_native
  Run: ./etpl_native --verify
  Expected: native binary passes all tests; no OS calls beyond syscall

Phase 8: Bare Metal Validation
  Flash etpl_native to test target (x86-64 bare metal)
  Expected: runs without OS; prints "P∘D∘T = EIM = S\nSelf-hosting: ✓"
```

---

## 17. Self-Hosting Milestone Map

| Milestone | Description | Criterion | Status |
|---|---|---|---|
| M0 | Zero-import invariant | No stdlib imports in any .py | 🔲 |
| M1 | ET theory library complete | et_verify_all() passes | 🔲 |
| M2 | Lexer+Parser functional | Tokenises and parses all ETPL syntax | 🔲 |
| M3 | Interpreter functional | Runs etpl_main.pdt via Python | 🔲 |
| M4 | Compiler produces .etb | Valid .etb with magic ETB1 | 🔲 |
| M5 | VM executes .etb | All tests pass from .etb | 🔲 |
| M6 | Self-compilation | etpl.etb compiles etpl_main.pdt → etpl2.etb | 🔲 |
| M7 | Binary determinism | etpl.etb == etpl2.etb | 🔲 |
| M8 | Native x86-64 | Native binary passes all tests on Linux | 🔲 |
| M9 | Native Windows | Native binary passes all tests on Windows | 🔲 |
| M10 | Bare metal | Runs without OS on x86-64 | 🔲 |
| M11 | ARM64 | Native binary on ARM64 | 🔲 |
| M12 | Full self-hosting | All .py archived; .pdt + .etb only | 🔲 |

